<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Gate^Flame mobile companion — network security policy.

  The app talks to exactly one thing: a Gate^Flame node on the customer's own
  LAN, over plain HTTP. That is unavoidable — the node has no public DNS name,
  so it cannot hold a publicly-trusted TLS certificate.

  ── WHY THIS FILE LOOKS BLUNTER THAN THE ONE IT REPLACES ────────────────────

  The previous version tried to permit cleartext only to RFC1918 by listing
  `192.168.1.0`, `10.0.0.0` and so on with includeSubdomains="true", on the
  stated assumption that this covered each range.

  It does not. Android's `<domain>` rule is a **hostname** match — exact, or a
  DNS-label suffix when includeSubdomains is set. It has no CIDR support at
  all. `<domain includeSubdomains="true">192.168.1.0</domain>` matches the
  literal host `192.168.1.0` and anything ending in `.192.168.1.0`. It does
  NOT match `192.168.1.105`: `105` is a sibling label, not a subdomain.

  So under the old policy, every request to a node by IP was rejected before
  it left the handset:

      CLEARTEXT communication to 192.168.1.105 not permitted by
      network security policy

  Only `gateflame.local` and `localhost` ever worked, and only when mDNS
  resolved. Manual address entry could never have worked at all. There is no
  way to express "RFC1918 only" in this file, and pretending to is worse than
  not trying: it reads as a narrow policy while actually being a broken one.

  ── WHAT PROTECTS THE CUSTOMER INSTEAD ──────────────────────────────────────

  The private-range restriction is enforced in the layer that can express it —
  `assertPrivateHost()` in src/services/apiClient.ts refuses any cleartext
  request whose host is not RFC1918, RFC3927 link-local, loopback, or a
  `.local` mDNS name. It is applied to every request the app makes and is
  covered by the test suite.

  The node enforces the same boundary independently: `security.py` gates every
  route on an RFC1918/loopback/link-local *source* address, so a request from
  off-LAN is refused by the node whatever the phone believes.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <!--
      Cleartext permitted. Android cannot scope this to private ranges; see
      above. Certificate trust is unchanged — system anchors only, no
      user-installed CAs, so an https:// endpoint added later still cannot be
      intercepted via a CA the customer was talked into installing.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

</network-security-config>
